# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DP Fitness · landing site environment
# Copy to `.env` (git-ignored). Unlike the member app, almost nothing here is
# public: the landing site is prerendered HTML with one server route behind it,
# and that route is the only thing that needs configuration.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the "Sign in" and "Register" calls-to-action send someone. The member
# app is a separate deployment, so this is an absolute origin in production.
NUXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# This site's own public origin, with no trailing slash — e.g.
# https://dpfitness.com. It is read at BUILD time, not at runtime, because the
# page is prerendered: it turns `/brand/og.png` into the absolute `og:image`
# that a link preview needs, and sets `og:url`.
#
# Leave it empty locally. Nothing breaks — the tags fall back to site-relative
# paths and most scrapers resolve them — but set it for a real deploy, or some
# platforms will unfurl a shared link without the card.
NUXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Selar — the payment
#
# Selar is a storefront, not a payments API. There is no call that starts a
# transaction and no call that asks whether one was paid, so the shape of this
# integration is dictated by what Selar does have: a hosted product page that
# can be pre-filled from a query string, and a sale notification it can be made
# to send somewhere.
#
# Four things have to be set up in the Selar dashboard, and the site does not
# work until all four are done.
#
#   1. THE PRODUCT. Create it, set the price to the same number `PRICE_MINOR`
#      in `app/data/landing.ts` prints on the page — ₦30,000 — and copy its
#      direct link. It looks like `https://selar.co/abc123`. Selar owns this
#      price now: changing it here changes what buyers are charged, and
#      changing PRICE_MINOR only changes what the page claims. Keep them equal.
#
#   2. THE REDIRECT. On that product, set the redirect-after-purchase URL to:
#
#          https://<your domain>/registration/complete
#
#      Selar appends nothing to it, which is fine — the page identifies the
#      buyer from a cookie this site set before sending them over.
#
#   3. THE NOTIFICATION. Selar has to POST every sale to:
#
#          https://<your domain>/api/payment/webhook
#
#      This is the only thing that issues an access code. If it is not wired
#      up, buyers pay and receive nothing at all.
#
#      Selar's supported route for this is Zapier (a paid Selar plan):
#      trigger "New Sale", filtered to the product above, then a "Webhooks by
#      Zapier → POST" action to that URL. Send JSON, and add a header
#      `X-Selar-Token` whose value is the secret below.
#
#      The handler reads whatever field names it recognises — `email`,
#      `customer_email`, `amount`, `product_name`, `purchase_code` and the
#      usual variants, at any nesting depth — so forwarding the whole trigger
#      payload works as well as hand-mapping fields. The one field it cannot do
#      without is the buyer's email address, because that is what matches a
#      sale back to a registration. A notification without one is answered 422
#      and the body is logged so the mapping can be fixed.
#
#   4. THE SECRET. Generate one and set it in both places — here, and as the
#      `X-Selar-Token` header value in the Zap.
#
#          openssl rand -hex 32
#
#      Selar signs nothing and can be asked nothing, so this token is the only
#      thing standing between the webhook and anyone who can guess the URL.
#      Whoever has it can mint free seats. Treat it like a password, and rotate
#      it in both places at once.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NUXT_SELAR_PRODUCT_URL=
NUXT_SELAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Locally there is no public URL for Selar or Zapier to reach, so no sale can
# be fulfilled by the real flow. To exercise it end to end, post a sale to the
# dev server by hand:
#
#   curl -X POST http://localhost:3001/api/payment/webhook \
#     -H 'content-type: application/json' \
#     -H "X-Selar-Token: $NUXT_SELAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET" \
#     -d '{"email":"you@example.com","amount":"30000","currency":"NGN"}'
#
# Register through the form first, with that same address — the sale is matched
# to the registration by email.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Firebase Admin credential — NOT public
#
# Note the missing `PUBLIC`. This is a private key that acts as the project
# owner and bypasses every security rule, so it must never be named
# NUXT_PUBLIC_*: that would compile it into the browser bundle, and there is no
# revoking a key that has shipped in a JS file.
#
# `server/api/register.post.ts` is the only thing that reads it, and it needs
# it because `firestore.rules` allows `create` on `accessCodes` only to a
# coach. A visitor filling in the registration form is not signed in at all, so
# there is no client-side path to issuing them a code that does not also let
# anyone in the world mint themselves a free seat.
#
#   Console → Project settings → Service accounts → Generate new private key
#   base64 -i service-account.json | tr -d '\n'
#
# Base64 rather than raw JSON because a PEM private key contains newlines and a
# .env value cannot span lines. Paste the result as one unbroken line. A
# hosting dashboard that accepts multi-line values takes the raw JSON too.
NUXT_FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=

# Alternative to the above: a path to the unencoded key file. Keep it outside
# the repo.
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/absolute/path/to/service-account.json

# Which Firestore database in the project. Leave empty for `(default)`, which
# is the one a project starts with. A second database (`staging`, say) is a
# separate database with its own documents AND ITS OWN RULES.
#
# Set this per environment, never from NODE_ENV: NODE_ENV is `production` for
# any built bundle, so a staging deploy keyed off it would write to production.
NUXT_FIREBASE_DATABASE_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Registration
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The cohort a self-serve registration joins. It must name a cohort that really
# exists: the member-create rule re-reads the access code document and refuses
# to write a member into a cohort the code does not name, so a code pointing at
# a missing cohort is a seat that can never be redeemed. `cohortName` and the
# program pin are read off that document rather than configured here, so they
# cannot drift from it.
NUXT_REGISTRATION_COHORT_ID=cohort-01

# How long an issued code stays redeemable, in days. The claim rule refuses a
# past `expiresAt`, so this is the window somebody has to actually sign up.
NUXT_REGISTRATION_CODE_TTL_DAYS=60

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Brevo — the access-code email
#
# The one email this site sends: the access code, to the address somebody just
# registered with. Registration works without any of this — the code is shown on
# the page either way — but that page render is then the only copy they get.
#
# NOT the sign-in email. The magic link that signs a member in is sent by
# Firebase's own mailer, is configured in the Firebase console, and lives in the
# member app. Nothing here touches it, and it is not to be moved onto Brevo.
#
# It must be a v3 API key, which starts `xkeysib-`. The SMTP key on the tab
# beside it starts `xsmtpsib-` and is for the SMTP relay, not this REST API —
# hand it to `/v3/smtp/email` and Brevo answers `401 Key not found`, which reads
# like a typo and sends you off to regenerate the wrong credential. The route
# checks the prefix and says so rather than letting the 401 mislead.
#
#   Brevo → SMTP & API → API Keys → Generate a new API key
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NUXT_BREVO_API_KEY=

# The From address. It must be a sender Brevo has verified, or every send is
# refused with a 400 that looks exactly like a bad API key — the reason
# `sendAccessCodeEmail` logs Brevo's response body rather than just the status.
NUXT_BREVO_SENDER_EMAIL=
NUXT_BREVO_SENDER_NAME=DP Fitness

# Where replies go, if that is not the sender. Optional. Either a bare address
# or the usual `Support <support@example.com>` form — the route splits the
# second into the two fields Brevo wants, because sent whole it fails address
# validation with a 400 that never mentions the angle brackets. The sender above
# accepts both forms too.
NUXT_BREVO_REPLY_TO=

# OPTIONAL, and an either/or rather than a customisation. Leave it empty —
# which is the default — and the designed HTML email in
# `server/emails/access-code.ts` is what sends, along with its plain-text part.
#
# Set it to the numeric id of a template authored in Brevo (Campaigns →
# Templates) and that template sends INSTEAD: the HTML above is not used at
# all. The only thing it buys is letting the copy change without a deploy.
#
# The parameters go out either way, so a Brevo template can read them with no
# code change:
#
#   {{ params.FIRST_NAME }}   {{ params.FULL_NAME }}
#   {{ params.ACCESS_CODE }}  {{ params.APP_URL }}
NUXT_BREVO_TEMPLATE_ID=
